title:: BMSpeakerSpec
summary:: Documentation BEASTmulch - specification for a speaker model
categories:: Classes

Description::

BMSpeakerSpec represents specifications for a particular speaker model. In addition to information on things like frequency response and type of speaker, it can also specify plugin presets for use with a speaker model (e.g. LPF input to a subwoofer), and can be extended to include arbitrary keys. User code can query a speaker's spec to find out information about its characteristics. A number of specs are created on startup, but users can add more.

See also: link::Classes/Inputs and Outputs/BMSpeaker::, link::Classes/Plugins/BMPlugin::, link::Classes/Plugins/BMTrimPluginsRack::

Section:: Specifications Dictionary

BMSpeakerSpecs take an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: or subclass (usually an link::Classes/Event::) to hold their specifications. Messages sent to BMSpeakerSpec which are not understood are forwarded to the specifications dictionary. Because of this BMSpeakerSpec can be generally treated as a link::Classes/Dictionary:: subclass, although it is not.

The specifications dictionary's proto var will be set to the following Event, thus setting a few default values:

code::
(brand: 'Unknown', fullRange: true);
::

Standard keys used in the specifications dictionary are as follows:

code::\brand::: A Symbol indicating the brand or manufacturer of this speaker model, e.g. 'Genelec'
code::\minFreq::: Minimum frequency response (typically free field +/- 2dB, but manufacturer's practices vary)
code::\maxFreq::: Maximum frequency response (typically free field +/- 2dB, but manufacturer's practices vary)
code::\spl::: dB SPL (typically continuous 1W @ 1m for unpowered, maximum continuous @ 1m for powered)
code::\powered::: A Boolean indicating if this speaker model is active or not
code::\fullRange::: A Boolean indicating if this speaker model is nominally 'full frequency range' rather than a specialised model such as a subwoofer or tweeter array
code::\plugins::: An Array of Arrays containing Symbols in the form [[\pluginName, \pluginPresetName], ...]. These are used in BMTrimPluginsRack's autoPlugins method. You will need to create the plugin presets themselves separately.
	
User code should use these keys rather than synonyms, but custom keys may be added as desired.

Note that in principle many or all of these values may be nil, and that user code may need to allow for this. 

CLASSMETHODS::

method:: new	
Create a new BMSpeakerSpec. This will overwrite any existing spec with the same name.

argument:: name
A link::Classes/Symbol:: indicating the name of this speaker spec, e.g. '8030A' or 'Generic Sub'.
argument:: vals
An link::Classes/IdentityDictionary:: or subclass containing keys and values for this model's specification.
		
code::
BMSpeakerSpec('8030A', (brand: 'Genelec', minFreq: 58, maxFreq: 20000, spl: 97, powered: true, fullRange: true)); // make a spec for a Genelec 8030A
::


INSTANCEMETHODS::
	
method:: specs
Returns an IdentityDictionary containing all existing specs with names as keys.
	
code::
BMSpeakerSpec.specs.postln; // see all available
BMSpeakerSpec.specs['UREI-809'] // get the Urei 809 spec
::

				
method:: name
Returns this spec's name as a link::Classes/Symbol::.

method:: doesNotUnderstand
Overrides the default method to forward any unimplemented method calls to the specifications dictionary.
		
argument:: selector
argument:: args

		// make a new spec
		g = BMSpeakerSpec(\foo, (minFreq: 20, maxFreq: 20000, spl: 99, powered: true, fullRange: true));
		g.minFreq; // forwards to spec dictionary and returns 20
		g.brand; // we didn't specify so we get the value from proto
		g.brand = 'Foobar Industries'; // we can set too
		g.brand;


